    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1027">

                <pb facs="1185" n="1073" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg1073"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">894.628/118</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Secretary of the
                        Navy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Swanson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-01-26">January 26, 1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I acknowledge the receipt
                    of your letter of January 19, 1935 (reference (SC)QS1/EF37), bringing to my
                    attention certain considerations arising out of the fact that Japanese fishing
                    vessels engaged in fishing on the Pacific Coast are equipped with long range
                    radio.</p>
                <p>An officer of this Department has brought this matter informally to the attention
                    of the secretary of the interdepartmental committee that has been set up to
                    consider ways and means to regulate vessels in the fisheries and it is
                    understood that the secretary will present the matter to the committee for its
                    consideration.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Cordell Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
